# Swift Stratified Files

Swift mirror of the Rust Layer-heading discipline (`languages/rust-stratified-modules`,
236 — lands with miniforge-standards#85) and its strict semantics. Complements swift.mdc (240)'s cross-file L0-L3 view
decomposition: 240 governs which FILE a declaration belongs in; this
rule governs the strata WITHIN a file. Applies
`foundations/stratified-design` (001) at file granularity.

## Marker

```swift
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 0
func vocab() -> Int { 1 }
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 1
func top() -> Int { vocab() }
```

- Comment heading: `//`, optional whitespace, ≥4 dashes, then `Layer N`
  — identical to the Clojure/Rust convention. Minimal canonical form:
  `// ---- Layer 0`; longer dash runs are purely visual.
- **Calls are strict**: a reference whose target is a same-file `func`
  must come from a strictly higher layer (SL008).
- **Vocabulary is lenient**: references to same-file types and top-level
  bindings are legal at the referencer's own layer; only upward
  references violate (SL001).
- **Types sit at their minimal layer** (SL009).
- Headings strictly ascend; **≤3 layers per file**; a fourth band or a
  long same-abstraction call chain is the split signal.

## What layering governs in Swift

Per-file lint covers **top-level declarations**. Member access is not a
reference edge, so methods inside a type or extension are invisible —
which means a god-class with headings is decoration, not discipline.
The honest structure, and exactly what 240 already prescribes:

- **Pure logic lives in top-level functions** in stratified files
  (240's L0 display mappers / L1 domain logic), where the linter has
  jurisdiction.
- **Stateful shells** (`ObservableObject` view models, coordinators)
  keep state and orchestration, delegate computation to those
  functions, and carry a single Layer heading for their one
  declaration.
- `extension` blocks attribute to their extended type — the Rust `impl`
  analog: non-referenceable, SL009-exempt, outgoing call edges checked.

## Enforcement

[`stratum-lint`](https://github.com/miniforge-ai/stratum-lint)'s Swift
implementation (`swift/`, SwiftPM, SwiftSyntax):

```bash
cd swift && swift run stratum-lint-swift <paths>
```

SL000 parse failure, SL001 upward reference, SL002 non-monotonic
headings, SL003 layer budget, SL004 declaration before the first
heading, SL008 same-layer call, SL009 over-placed type. Files without
headings are skipped (adoption affordance, not an exemption).
Limitations mirroring the other linters: same-file method calls and
local shadowing of a top-level name are not resolved — review covers
them.

## Exemplar

`thesium-app/Sources/RiskAppleApp/DashboardViewModel.swift` — a
1,008-line file (seven top-level types + a 930-line view model) split
into a 791-line stateful shell over five Layer-headed files
(`PortfolioDisplayModes`, `PortfolioGrouping`, `PortfolioSummaries`,
`PortfolioImportPayload`, `SnapshotOrdering`), behavior-exact, all 197
package tests unmodified and green, strict-lint clean.

## Examples

<example>
  Good — vocabulary below, pure domain function above, shell delegates:

  ```swift
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 0
  struct AccountGroup: Identifiable { /* ... */ }
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 1
  func groupedByAccount(_ holdings: [PortfolioHolding]) -> [AccountGroup] { /* ... */ }
  ```
</example>

<example type="invalid">
  ```swift
  // BAD — same-layer func call: caller must sit strictly above  // SL008
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 0
  func headingLayer(_ line: Substring) -> Int? { /* ... */ }
  func headings(_ source: String) -> [Heading] { source.split(...).compactMap(headingLayer) }

  // BAD — god-class with headings but no extracted functions:
  // methods are invisible to per-file lint; extract pure logic
  // into top-level functions per swift.mdc 240.
  ```
</example>
